I definitely have to agree with this.  I would also emphasize playing
a character who starts with intrinsic speed.  This way you get more
moves in before your food runs out.  A monk is a good choice for this
if you want both, because you get poison resistance at level 3. 
Barbarians are also a good choice.

> And remember: If you keep the gods happy in good times, they will help you
> in bad times.

For somebody who has problems starving, praying is the best way out of
it.  Remember what the turn clock said when you prayed.  If you wait
at least 1000 (this is not precise, but it's an estimate I use) turns
after your last prayer, you're usually in good shape to pray again
when you are weak or fainting.  This will keep you alive
nigh-indefinitely and easily as long as you have eaten a minor amount
of food to keep you from fainting before that time.

A more specific strategy: Don't eat any food that doesn't spoil (food
rations, fruits, tripe, tins, lichen corpses, etc...) until you
absolutely have to.

(Non-lichen) corpses spoil after a number of turns, so consider them
an immediate source of food.  Try to eat corpses to stay satiated and
accumulate a food reserve of non-spoiling food in your inventory as a
buffer against starvation.  Leave your pet behind (but don't kill it)
because it tends to eat food you would have wanted.  If you do get
weak and have no more corpses and you can pray again, do that instead
of eating your food rations.  Only if you can't pray and are weak
should you eat something from your inventory.  I wouldn't consider
this an ideal way to play the game, but it's definitely a good way not
to starve.

